# Fingerprints on relations

A reference ties one place in a book to another. An anchor in code composes a section, and an anchor in prose links to one. The author who wrote the reference read the section it points to as that section stood on the day. When the section changes later, nothing tells the author that the reference may no longer hold.

This chapter gives each such tie a fingerprint. Loom records what a section looked like when the author last approved a reference to it, and it reports the reference as drifted once the section no longer matches. So no code and no prose drifts in silence from a section it depends on.

## A relation

A relation is one tie, from a reference to the section it points to. It is found by Loom's own names: the `loom` and the `section` that hold the reference, the `kind` of reference, and the name the reference writes, which the relation keeps as `to`. Beside those names it holds one fact, the `fingerprint` of the section as the author approved it.

=>

export const RelationSchema = Schema.Struct({
  loom: Schema.String,
  section: Schema.String,
  kind: Schema.String,
  to: Schema.String,
  fingerprint: Schema.String,
})
export type Relation = typeof RelationSchema.Type

## The references that make relations

Two kinds of reference make a relation. An `anchor` is an anchor in code, which composes a section or names its module. A `link` is an anchor in prose, which links to a section. A box that points to a chapter makes none, since the box already copies a summary of what it points to and goes stale with it, as the chapter "Boxes" describes.

A reference makes a relation only when it points into another loom. Inside one loom the prose and the code of every section sit together under the author's eyes, and a section that assembles a file refers to every section it composes. A fingerprint there would mark those references at every edit.

A `Reference` is one reference found in a loom: its kind, the section that holds it, the name it writes, where it stands, and the section it points to. The kinds are a table, `relationKinds`, and each entry holds a kind's name and how to find its references in a loom. A further kind of tie joins by adding an entry. `referencesOf` reads every reference of a loom through the table.

=>

export type Reference = {
  readonly kind: string
  readonly section: string
  readonly to: string
  readonly at: Position
  readonly target: SectionRef
}

type Found = Omit<Reference, 'kind'>

export type RelationKind = {
  readonly name: string
  readonly referencesIn: (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule) => ReadonlyArray<Found>
}

const inAnotherLoom =
  (module: LoomModule) =>
  (target: SectionRef): boolean =>
    target.module !== module.path

~

An anchor in code reaches the product as an edge. An edge the loom bound to one of its own sections stays inside the loom, so `anchorsIn` passes it by. Any other edge resolves through the book, as the chapter "The product pass" describes, and `openEdge` keeps it when it resolves to one section of another loom.

=>

type Edge = {
  readonly name: string
  readonly target: Option.Option<unknown>
  readonly anchor: Position
}

const openEdge =
  (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule, section: string) =>
  (edge: Edge): ReadonlyArray<Found> =>
    Option.isSome(edge.target)
      ? []
      : pipe(
          resolveName(catalogue, module.path, edge.name),
          Option.filter(inAnotherLoom(module)),
          Option.map((target): Found => ({ section, to: edge.name, at: edge.anchor, target })),
          Option.toArray,
        )

const anchorsIn = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Found> =>
  Array.flatMap(module.product.code, (host) =>
    Array.flatMap(host.fragments, (part) =>
      Match.value(part).pipe(
        Match.when({ type: 'NameRef' }, openEdge(catalogue, module, host.origin.name)),
        Match.when({ type: 'ModuleRef' }, openEdge(catalogue, module, host.origin.name)),
        Match.when({ type: 'Fragment' }, (): ReadonlyArray<Found> => []),
        Match.exhaustive,
      ),
    ),
  )

~

A link stands in the prose of a section. `linksIn` resolves each link of each section and keeps the ones that point into another loom.

=>

const linksIn = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Found> =>
  Array.flatMap(module.doc.sections, (section) =>
    Array.flatMap(sectionLinksOf(section), (link) =>
      pipe(
        resolveAnchor(catalogue, module.path, link),
        Option.filter(inAnotherLoom(module)),
        Option.map(
          (target): Found => ({
            section: sectionNameOf(section),
            to: link.name.value,
            at: link.name.position,
            target,
          }),
        ),
        Option.toArray,
      ),
    ),
  )

export const relationKinds: ReadonlyArray<RelationKind> = [
  { name: 'anchor', referencesIn: anchorsIn },
  { name: 'link', referencesIn: linksIn },
]

export const referencesOf = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
): ReadonlyArray<Reference> =>
  Array.flatMap(relationKinds, (kind) =>
    Array.map(kind.referencesIn(catalogue, module), (found) => ({ ...found, kind: kind.name })),
  )

## A fingerprint says whether, never where

The fingerprint of a section is a hash of the section's text, its prose and its code together. Blank lines and spaces at the end of a line do not count, so a section that was only tidied keeps its fingerprint. `fingerprintOf` hashes the text that `settled` gives.

A fingerprint answers one question: is this still the text the author approved. It never finds anything. Tokens say where a reference stands, and a relation's names say which relation it is.

=>

const settled = (text: string): string =>
  pipe(
    text.split('\n'),
    Array.map((line) => line.trimEnd()),
    Array.filter((line) => line !== ''),
    Array.join('\n'),
  )

export const fingerprintOf = (section: LoomSection): string =>
  createHash('sha256').update(settled(section.source)).digest('hex')

## Drift

A relation has drifted when the section it points to no longer matches the fingerprint the relation holds. `driftFaults` gives each drifted reference of a loom the `DriftedReference` fault, on the reference itself. The fault is a warning. A tangle goes ahead all the same, since a drifted reference still composes.

A reference that nobody has approved has no relation, and Loom says nothing about it. So a book that starts to use fingerprints does not light up, and an author takes them up one loom at a time.

Loom keeps a fingerprint and no text, so it can say that a section changed and cannot say what changed. The author reads the section, or asks version control for the difference.

=>

const keyOf = (loom: Location, held: Pick<Relation, 'section' | 'kind' | 'to'>): string =>
  JSON.stringify([loom, held.section, held.kind, held.to])

export const driftFaults = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<Relation>,
): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => {
  const approved = new Map(
    Array.map(kept, (relation) => [keyOf(relation.loom, relation), relation.fingerprint] as const),
  )
  return Array.flatMap(referencesOf(catalogue, module), (reference) =>
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(approved.get(keyOf(module.path, reference))),
      Option.filter((fingerprint) => fingerprint !== fingerprintOf(reference.target.section)),
      Option.match({
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
        onSome: () => faulty(DriftedReference({ name: reference.to }), reference.at).diagnostics,
      }),
    ),
  )
}

## Approving

Approving a reference records the fingerprint of its section as the section stands now. The author has read the section and the reference still holds.

`approvedRelations` approves every reference of one loom. It gives the relations to keep: one for each reference the loom holds, and the relations of every other loom as they were. A relation of this loom whose reference is gone is not among them, so what is kept holds the references that exist and no history. Two references in one section that write the same name tie the same two places and make one relation. The relations come sorted by their names, so two authors who approve different looms change different lines of the file that keeps them.

=>

const byNames: Order.Order<Relation> = Order.mapInput(Order.String, (relation: Relation) =>
  keyOf(relation.loom, relation),
)

export const approvedRelations = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<Relation>,
): ReadonlyArray<Relation> =>
  pipe(
    [
      ...Array.filter(kept, (relation) => relation.loom !== module.path),
      ...Array.dedupeWith(
        Array.map(
          referencesOf(catalogue, module),
          (reference): Relation => ({
            loom: module.path,
            section: reference.section,
            kind: reference.kind,
            to: reference.to,
            fingerprint: fingerprintOf(reference.target.section),
          }),
        ),
        (one: Relation, other: Relation) => keyOf(one.loom, one) === keyOf(other.loom, other),
      ),
    ],
    Array.sort(byNames),
  )

## What relations draw on

The corpus model gives the catalogue, a loom's module, the links of a section, and the two functions that resolve a name and an anchor to a section. The product pass gives a section's name, and the fault catalog gives `faulty` and the `DriftedReference` fault. Node's `createHash` computes the hash. Effect supplies `Schema` for the relation, and `Array`, `Match`, `Option`, `Order`, and `pipe` for the rest.

=>

import { createHash } from 'node:crypto'
import { Array, Match, Option, Order, pipe, Schema } from 'effect'
import type { Diagnostic, Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import type { LoomSection } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import {
  type Catalogue,
  type Location,
  type LoomModule,
  resolveAnchor,
  resolveName,
  sectionLinksOf,
  type SectionRef,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { DriftedReference, faulty } from '#ast/LoomFault'
import { sectionNameOf } from '#ast/ProductBuilder'

